    "m2_prompt_trader_proposal_original": "**Action**: Hold\n\n**Reasoning**: The evidence presents a genuinely balanced picture for JPM. The bear case wins the short-term technical argument decisively: a 6.5% one-day drop on 3.5x average volume (31.5M shares, ~$5.5B notional) signals high-conviction institutional selling, price broke below the 50-day SMA ($178.05) and the lower Bollinger Band, closed just 24 cents off its low, and the 5-day return is -7.42% with a 20-day return of -2.19% \u2014 a sustained decline, not a one-day event. However, the bull case wins the medium-to-long-term fundamental argument: JPM closed at $174.45, still 14% above a rising 200-day SMA at $152.73, with a fortress balance sheet ($3.875T assets, $328B equity, $55B FY2023 net cash generation, $25.6B returned to shareholders). RSI at 33.5 is approaching but not yet oversold (threshold 30), so capitulation is unproven. With the Q1 earnings catalyst imminent and no income statement data available to confirm forward profitability, buying into a falling knife before earnings is not compelling, but selling a fortress-balance-sheet bank trading 14% above its rising long-term trend is equally uncompelling. This is a genuine Hold \u2014 maintain the position with a protective stop at $170, do not add new exposure until price reclaims the 50-day SMA at $178 on declining volume, RSI reverses from oversold, or the earnings print provides clarity.\n\n**Entry Price**: 174.45\n\n**Stop Loss**: 170.0\n\n**Position Sizing**: Maintain neutral-to-slightly-underweight stance; do not exceed full target weight until price stabilizes above $178\n\nFINAL TRANSACTION PROPOSAL: **HOLD**",
